      ":question/id": "208ff351-e5d0-57cc-aa80-c17be974dd3e",
      ":question/math-academy-id": "q-332169",
      ":question/problem": "Express $\\operatorname{sin}(x+{210}^{∘})$ in terms of $\\operatorname{sin}x$ and $\\operatorname{cos}x.$\n\n$\\operatorname{sin}(x+{210}^{∘})=$\n{{field-1}}$\\operatorname{sin}x\\,-$\n{{field-2}}$\\operatorname{cos}x$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Recall the sum formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}(u+v)=\\operatorname{sin}u\\operatorname{cos}v+\\operatorname{cos}u\\operatorname{sin}v\n$$\n\nApplying the sum formula for sine with $u=x$ and $v={210}^{∘},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}(x+{210}^{∘}) & =\\operatorname{sin}x\\operatorname{cos}{210}^{∘}+\\operatorname{cos}x\\operatorname{sin}{210}^{∘} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}x\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)+\\operatorname{cos}x\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\,\\operatorname{sin}x-\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\operatorname{cos}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\,\\operatorname{sin}x-\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\operatorname{cos}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"
