      },
      ":question/id": "4d725a40-8d02-5848-a5ca-6afc87538588",
      ":question/problem": "Express $\\sin(x+150^\\circ)-\\sin(x-45^\\circ)$ in the form $A\\sin x+B\\cos x$. Enter exact, signed coefficients.\n\n$A=$ {{field-1}}\n\n$B=$ {{field-2}}",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Use the sum and difference formulas for sine:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sin(u+v)&=\\sin u\\cos v+\\cos u\\sin v,\\\\\n\\sin(u-v)&=\\sin u\\cos v-\\cos u\\sin v.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nThe needed exact values are\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\cos150^\\circ&=-\\frac{\\sqrt3}{2}, & \\sin150^\\circ&=\\frac12,\\\\\n\\cos45^\\circ&=\\frac{\\sqrt2}{2}, & \\sin45^\\circ&=\\frac{\\sqrt2}{2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nExpand each sine expression:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sin(x+150^\\circ)&=-\\frac{\\sqrt3}{2}\\sin x+\\frac12\\cos x,\\\\\n\\sin(x-45^\\circ)&=\\frac{\\sqrt2}{2}\\sin x-\\frac{\\sqrt2}{2}\\cos x.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nLet $E$ denote the original expression. Subtract the entire second expansion, reversing both of its signs, and collect the sine and cosine terms:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE&=\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt3}{2}\\sin x+\\frac12\\cos x\\right)\\\\\n &\\quad-\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt2}{2}\\sin x-\\frac{\\sqrt2}{2}\\cos x\\right)\\\\\n &=\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt3}{2}-\\frac{\\sqrt2}{2}\\right)\\sin x\\\\\n &\\quad+\\left(\\frac12+\\frac{\\sqrt2}{2}\\right)\\cos x\\\\\n &=-\\frac{\\sqrt3+\\sqrt2}{2}\\sin x+\\frac{1+\\sqrt2}{2}\\cos x.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nThus $A=-\\frac{\\sqrt3+\\sqrt2}{2}$ and $B=\\frac{1+\\sqrt2}{2}$."
    },
