      ":question/difficulty": {
        ":db/ident": ":question.difficulty/hard"
      },
      ":question/id": "ebc66527-3ffa-5a2f-9b76-a34ac24b5bd7",
      ":question/problem": "Express $\\sin(x+135^\\circ)+\\sin(x+45^\\circ)$ in the form $A\\sin x+B\\cos x$. Enter exact, signed coefficients.\n\n$A=$ {{field-1}}\n\n$B=$ {{field-2}}",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Use the sum formula for sine:\n$$\n\\sin(u+v)=\\sin u\\cos v+\\cos u\\sin v.\n$$\nUse the sum formula for sine:\n$$\n\\sin(u+v)=\\sin u\\cos v+\\cos u\\sin v.\n$$\nExpand the two terms separately:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sin(x+135^\\circ)&=\\sin x\\cos 135^\\circ+\\cos x\\sin 135^\\circ\\\\\n&=\\sin x\\cdot\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)+\\cos x\\cdot\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)\\\\\n&=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\sin x+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\cos x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sin(x+45^\\circ)&=\\sin x\\cos 45^\\circ+\\cos x\\sin 45^\\circ\\\\\n&=\\sin x\\cdot\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)+\\cos x\\cdot\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)\\\\\n&=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\sin x+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\cos x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nLet $E$ denote the original expression. Add the entire second expansion and collect like terms:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE&=\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}+\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)\\right)\\sin x\\\\\n&\\quad+\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}+\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)\\right)\\cos x\\\\\n&=\\left(0\\right)\\sin x\\\\\n&\\quad+\\left(\\sqrt{2}\\right)\\cos x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nThus $A=0$ and $B=\\sqrt{2}$."
    }
  ],
  "canonical_example": {
    ":db/id": 17592186059493,
    ":question/id": "4cd24c10-1838-5f4b-ac88-cfbfc2ec4c21",
    ":question/math-academy-id": "e-3518",
    ":question/problem": "Express $\\sin\\left(x - 30^{\\circ} \\right )$ in terms of $\\sin x$ and $\\cos x.$",
    ":question/worked-solution": "Recall the difference formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\sin(u-v) = \\sin u\\cos v - \\cos u\\sin v\n$$\n\nApplying the difference formula for sine with $u=x$ and $v=30^{\\circ},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-{30}^{∘}) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡{30}^{∘}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡{30}^{∘} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\cdot \\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{2}-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"
  },
